<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The **neuron doctrine** is a fundamental theory in neuroscience that states that neurons are the basic structural and functional units of the nervous system. This means that neurons are independent, self-contained units that communicate with other neurons through synapses, rather than being a continuous network of protoplasmic fluid. This theory explains how the nervous system functions by explaining that neurons are not connected end-to-end, but instead communicate through specialized connections.

The neuron doctrine was not a single discovery but a result of the work of several scientists, with **Santiago Ramón y Cajal** being considered the most significant contributor. However, the development of the theory was driven by a debate with **Comito Golgi**, which led to a better understanding of the nervous system.
