## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
What is the amount of heat released when 2.5 moles of propane (C3H8) is completely burned in oxygen gas (O2) according to the balanced equation: 

C3H8 + 5O2 → 3CO2 + 4H2O 

given that the standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2220 kJ/mol?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The balanced reaction is:
C3H8 + 5O2 → 3CO2 + 4H2O  
The standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2220 kJ/mol. This means that 1 mole of propane releases 2220 kJ of heat when completely burned.  
The question asks for the heat released when 2.5 moles of propane are burned.  
Since 1 mole of propane releases 2220 kJ, then 2.5 moles of propane will release:  
\[
\text{Heat released} = 2.5 \text{ mol} \times 2220 \text{ kJ/mol} = 5550 \text{ kJ}
\]  
The amount of heat released is $\boxed{5550 \text{ kJ}}$.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

