","The system reliability function \( R_S(t) \) is the probability that the system survives time \( t \). For non-repairable systems, the failure rate is constant, and the failure rate of each component is \( p_i = \frac{1}{ \lambda_i } \), where \(\lambda_i\) is the lifetime of the component \(i\). The reliability of each component is \( R_i(t) = 1 - p_i t = 1 - \frac{1}{\lambda_i} t \).

The formula for \( R_S(t) \) depends on the system configuration.

---

### a) Sequential (Serial) Structure  
In a serial system, the system fails if any component fails. The reliability of the system is the probability that all components survive. Since the components are independent, the reliability of the system is the sum of the reliability of each component.

\[
R_S(t) = R_1(t) R_2(t) R_3(t) \quad \text{For 3 components.}
