- Step I: \( \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{Cl} \) (fast, equilibrium)
- Step II: \( \text{Cl} + \text{CO} \rightarrow \text{COCl} \) (fast, equilibrium)
- Step III: \( \text{COCl} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COCl}_2 + \text{Cl} \) (slow)

What is the molecularity of the rate-determining step (Step III)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The rate-determining step is the slowest step in a reaction mechanism, which is Step III in this case:
\[ \text{COCl} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COCl}_2 + \text{Cl} \]
The mechanism involves the reaction of one molecule of \( \text{COCl} \) and one molecule of \( \text{Cl}_2 \). The molecularity is defined as the number of molecules reacting in the elementary reaction. Since two molecules (\(\text{COCl}\) and \(\text{Cl}_2\)) react in Step III, the mechanism is **bimolecular**.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

