","To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the following formula:

\[
\Delta H = -[ \Delta H_i + \Delta H_2 + \Delta H_3 ] + [ \Delta H_4 + \Delta H_5 + \Delta H_5 ]
\]

where:
- \(\Delta H\) is the standard enthalpy change for the reaction.
- \(\Delta H_1\) is the standard enthalpy of formation of the reactant \(CaO\).
- \(\Delta H_2\) is the standard enthalpy of formation of the reactant \(H_2O\).
- \(\Delta H_3\) is the standard enthalpy of formation of the product \(Ca(OH]_2\).

In this case, the balanced chemical equation is:
\[
CaO(s] + H_2O(l) \rightarrow Ca(OH]_2(s)
\]
The standard enthalpies of formation are:
- \(\Delta H_1 = \Delta H_f(CaO(s]) = -635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}\)
- \(\Delta H_2 = \Delta H_f(H_2O(l)) = -285.8 \, \text{kJ/mol}\)
- \(\Delta H_3 = \Delta H_f(Ca(OH]_2(s)) = -986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}\)

Using the heat of formation calculation:
\[
\Delta H = - ( \Delta H_f(CaO(s]) + \Delta H_f(H_2O(l)) ) + \Delta H_f(Ca(OH]_2(s)])
\]
\[
\Delta H = - (- 635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol} + - 285.8 \, \text{kJ/mol}) + - 986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}
\]
\[
\Delta H = - (- 921.9 \, \text{kJ/mol}) - 986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}
