","To prove that for integers \(a, b \in \mathbb{J}\), \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\) if and only if \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\), we need to verify the two directions of the statement.

1. If \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\), then \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\).
If \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\), then \(a =b + 10 \cdot n\) for some integer \(n\).
Since \(10 = 2 \cdot 5\), we can write \(a = b + 2 \cdot 5 \cdot n = b + 2 \cdot n^2\) and \(a = b + 5 \cdot 2 \cdot n = b + 5 \cdot n^2\).
