"What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by the inverse of the difference between the free medium refractive index (\(n_f\)) and the effective refractive index (\(n_{\text{eff}}\)) of the core, multiplied by the wavelength (\(\lambda\)). The formula is:

\[
\tau = \frac{\lambda}{\Delta n}
\]

where:
- \(\tau\) is the decay length (thickness of the evanescent wave)
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the wave
- \(\Delta n =n_{\text{eff}} - n_f\) is the difference between the effective refractive index and the free medium refractive index.

In this case, we are given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \ \mu\text{m} = 1000 \ \mu\text{m}\)
- \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\)

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[
\tau = \frac{1000 \, \mu\text{m}}{0.3} = 3000 \, \mu\text{m} = 3 \, \text{mm}
\]

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{3 \ \text{mm}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
