<USER_REQUEST>
A particle of mass 30 kg is subjected to a force of 32 N. Calculate the acceleration of the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the acceleration of the particle, we use Newton's second law of motion, which states:
\[
a = \frac{F}{m}
\]
where:
\[
a = \text{acceleration}
\]
\[
F = \text{force}
text{
\[
m = \text{mass}
\]
In this case, the force \( F = 32 \, \text{N} \) and the mass \( m = 30 \, \text{ kg} \).
Thus, the acceleration is:
\[
a = \frac{32 \, \text{N}}{30 \, \text{kg}} = \frac{32}{30} \, \text{m/s}^2 = \frac{16}{15} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]
Rounding the acceleration, we have:
\[
a = 1.76 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

Thus, the acceleration of the particle is \(\frac{16}{15} \, \text{m/s}^2\) or \(\boxed{1.76 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

