<USER_REQUEST>
A cylinder contains 2 moles of an ideal gas at an initial pressure of 2 atm and an initial volume of 4L. The gas expands isothermally until its volume becomes 8L. Calculate the work done by the gas during the expansion process.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The work (ΔU) developed by a gas during an isothermal expansion is given by the formula:
\[
\DeltaU = P \DeltaV = P \times (V_f - V_i)
\]
where:
- \(P\) is the pressure of the gas during the expansion.
- \(V_f\) is the final volume of the gas.
- \(v_i\) is the initial volume of the gas.

Since the gas is at 2 atm, the pressure is constant during the isothermal expansion.
Given:
- Initial pressure (\(P_i\)) = 2 atm
- Initial volume (\(V_i\)) = 4 L
- Final volume (\(V_f\))) = 8 L

Thus, the change in volume (\(\DeltaV\)) is:
\[
\DeltaV = V_f - V_i = 8 \text{ L} - 4 \text{ L} = 4 \text{ L}
\]

The work done is:
\[
\DeltaU = P \times \DeltaV = 2 \text{ atm} \times 4 \text{ L} = 8 \text{ J}
\]

The work done by the gas during the expansion process is 8 J.

Final Answer:
\(\boxed{8 \text{ J}}\)
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

