","The total time taken for the objects to collide again after the initial collision depends on the direction of motion after the collision. The coefficient of elasticity \( e \) is defined as the ratio of the relative velocities after the collision to the relative velocities before the collision:
\[ e = \frac{v_2 - v_3}{v_1 - v_2} = \frac{\Delta{v}}{\Delta{v}^_-} \]
where \( v_1 \) and \( v_2 \) are the velocities of the two objects before the collision, and \( v_3 \) and \( v_4 \) are the velocities of the two objects after the collision.
The velocities before the collision are \( v_1 = v_0 \) and \( v_2 = 0 \).
The velocities after the collision are \( v_3 \) and \( v_4 \).
The coefficient of restitution is given as \( e \), so
\[ e = \frac{v_4 - v_3}{v0 - 0} = \frac{v_4 - v_3}{v_0} \]
Thus, \( v_4 - v_3 = e v_0 \).
The momentum of the two objects is conserved during the collision, so
\[ mv_1 + Mv_2 = mv_3 + Mv_4 = mv_3 + Mv_4 = mv_0 + 0 = mv_0 \]
Thus, \( mv_3 + Mv_4 = mv_0 \).
From \( v_4 - v_3 = e v_0 \), we have \( v_4 = v_3 + e v_0 \).
