","The tangential acceleration of a point on the rim of a rotating object is related to the net torque acting on the object by the equation:
\[ \tau = I \alpha \]
where:
- $\tau$ is the net torque on the object
- \( I \) is the moment of inertia of the object
- \( \alpha \) is the angular acceleration of the object

For a disc-like pulley, the moment of inertia is given by:
\[ I = \frac{1}{2} m R^2 \)
where:
- \( m \) is the mass of the pulley
- \( R \) is the radius of the pulley

In this case, \( m = 1 \, \text{kg} \) and \( R = 0.5 \, \text{m} \), so
\[ I = \frac{1}{2} ( 1 \, \text{kg}) ( 0.5 \, \text{m} ) = 0.25 \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^2 \)

The tangential acceleration \( a_t \) is related to the angular acceleration \( \alpha \) by:
\[ a_t = r \alpha \)
where \( r \) is the radius of the pulley.
Since \( a_t = 2 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) and \( R = 0.5 \, \text{m} \), we have:
\[ 2 \, \text{m/s}^2 = 0.5 \, \text{m} \times \alpha \]
Thus, \( \alpha = \frac{2 \, \text{m/s}^2}{0.5 \, \text{m}} = 4 \, \text{s}^2 \)

The torque due to the force \( F \) is given by:
\[ \tau = F R \)
Since the tension in the rope is negligible, the net torque is just due to the force \( F \). Thus,
\[ \tau = F R = I \alpha = 0.25 \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m}^2 \times 4 \, \text{s}^2 = 1 \, \text{Nm} \)
Thus,
\[ F R = 1 \, \text{Nm} \]
Since \( R = 0.5 \, \text{m} \),
\[ F ( 0.5 \, \text{m} ) = 1 \, \text{Nm} \]
Thus,
\[ F = \frac{1 \, \text{Nm}}{0.5 \, \text{m}} = 2 \, \text{N} \)

